Angkor's rice fields and reservoirs form a cultural landscape.

42
00:02:57.573 --> 00:03:00.829
Your turn. Say: cultural landscape.

43
00:03:03.529 --> 00:03:04.925
cultural landscape.

44
00:03:05.917 --> 00:03:08.032
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:08.332 --> 00:03:14.546
Ms. Sarah, by 2030, Angkor will have been a World Heritage Site for nearly forty years.

46
00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:20.729
And for all that time, experts will have been balancing tourism and protection.

47
00:03:22.017 --> 00:03:25.409
Is there a limit to how many visitors the temples can take?

48
00:03:26.717 --> 00:03:32.750
Yes. That's the site carrying capacity. Some steps have been closed to prevent erosion.

49
00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:36.758
There's also the authenticity debate about restoration.

50
00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:43.907
Right. Restoration ethics say repairs should be visible and reversible.

51
00:03:45.217 --> 00:03:48.180
And local villagers still live inside the park.

52
00:03:49.483 --> 00:03:53.866
Which makes it living heritage. Community stewardship is essential.

53
00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:58.949
Grammar focus: Future perfect continuous (mastery).

54
00:03:59.249 --> 00:04:02.131
Compare the future perfect continuous and simple.

55
00:04:02.431 --> 00:04:07.774
By 2030, experts will have been balancing tourism and protection for decades.

56
00:04:08.074 --> 00:04:14.091
By 2030, Angkor will have been a World Heritage Site for nearly forty years.

57
00:04:14.391 --> 00:04:20.721
Use the continuous form for ongoing effort; use the simple form for states and completed results.

58
00:04:21.717 --> 00:04:24.369
Now read and listen. Protecting Angkor.

59
00:04:24.669 --> 00:04:30.140
Angkor was inscribed on the World Heritage List in 1992.

60
00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:36.224
At that time, many temples were damaged and the site was considered at risk.

61
00:04:37.517 --> 00:04:43.162
Since then, international teams and Cambodian experts have restored major monuments.

62
00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:50.037
Heritage tourism has grown dramatically, bringing millions of visitors and significant income.

63
00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:56.751
However, large crowds can damage stone surfaces and strain water resources.

64
00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:04.624
Managers now consider the site carrying capacity and encourage visitors to explore less famous temples.

65
00:05:05.917 --> 00:05:12.073
Because people still live and worship there, Angkor is a living heritage and a cultural landscape.

66
00:05:13.383 --> 00:05:19.946
Its future will depend on community stewardship as much as on international expertise.

67
00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:24.251
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:24.551 --> 00:05:28.350
The number of visitors a site can receive without damage is its blank.

69
00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:35.066
A, site carrying capacity. B, heritage management. C, restoration ethics.

70
